We distinguish $\valoptim$ (resp. $\valpessim$) the optimistic (resp. pessimistic) value function, where $\valoptim(\firstvar) = + \infty$ (resp. $\valpessim(\firstvar) = - \infty$), if $\Theta(\firstvar) = \emptyset$.  We emphasize that the term ``value function'' is different from the ``lower-level value function'', which has been used in the literature to construct algorithms, starting with \cite{ye1995optimality}. For simplicity, we will focus on the optimistic formulation of the bilevel problem, for the rest of this paper, the term ``bilevel problem'' in \eqref{eq:simple-bilevel-poly-optim} denotes the optimistic formulation \eqref{eq:optimistic-bilevel} and $\valbilevel = \valoptim$ denote the corresponding value function. This amounts to considering that the minimization in \eqref{eq:simple-bilevel-poly-optim} also takes place over the variable $y$. The majority of our results have a counterpart for the pessimistic formulation, see \Cref{subsection:summary_results}, with lower semicontinuity is replaced by upper semicontinuity.
